#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Overview {#overview}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Quantum error mitigation (QEM) encompasses algorithmic protocols, such as "
"zero-noise extrapolation (ZNE) [[2]](#references) and probabilistic error "
"cancellation (PEC) [[3]](#references), that are designed to suppress noise-"
"induced bias in expectation values on noisy intermediate-scale quantum "
"(NISQ) hardware, without the large qubit overhead of full fault tolerance. "
"These methods are critical for extracting reliable physical simulations and "
"benchmark results from current processors. Although many QEM methods have "
"been developed in recent years, almost all of them share a fundamental "
"trade-off: they rely on significantly more QPU time to achieve accuracy. "
"Their defining limitation is an exponential sampling overhead, because "
"reconstructing an unbiased signal from noisy circuits greatly increases the "
"statistical variance of the estimator. Consequently, overcoming higher noise"
" levels requires orders of magnitude more measurement shots, which leads to "
"significantly longer QPU runtimes and substantial hardware bottlenecks [[4, "
"5, 6]](#references).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Diagram of the AI-QEM workflow. A user-provided Estimator PUB and IBM "
"backend coupling and calibration data feed into the AI-QEM circuit function,"
" which decomposes observables into Pauli strings, measures noisy Pauli "
"expectation values on IBM Quantum hardware, predicts correction factors with"
" an LTM AI model, and applies the corrections to compute mitigated "
"expectation values. A bar chart compares noisy, mitigated, and ideal values "
"for five observables.](/docs/images/guides/ltm-ai-qem/workflow.svg \"AI-QEM "
"workflow\")\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"With AI-QEM, you can run quantum circuits on noisy QPUs and obtain improved "
"expectation values compared to other AI-based error mitigation techniques, "
"without any additional QPU runtime overhead. The approach uses a deep neural"
" network with a graph convolutional network (GCN)-Transformer architecture. "
"The network is trained on features that include circuit structure, backend "
"topology, Pauli observables, and qubit calibrations, and it predicts a "
"correction factor, denoted by $\\zeta$. Dividing the noisy expectation value"
" by $\\zeta$ produces an improved expectation value. Experiments show that "
"AI-QEM produces better results than other AI-based mitigation methods, "
"particularly for circuits with large depth (gate layers) and width (number "
"of qubits), which makes it well suited for utility-scale quantum "
"experiments. The table in the [Benchmarks](#benchmarks) section shows that "
"the GCN-Transformer-based model surpasses random forest (RF)-based models in"
" the 80- to 150-qubit range, with a mean absolute error (MAE) of 0.343. The "
"MAE is the average, over a large number of circuit and observable "
"configurations, of the absolute difference between the ideal expectation "
"value and the expectation value after the model mitigates the noisy hardware"
" result. Furthermore, because the model is circuit-agnostic, you can apply "
"it to a wide range of quantum circuits and applications without circuit-"
"specific customization.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For a full description of the underlying components and a utility-scale "
"demonstration, refer to the AI-QEM paper [[1]](#references).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Description {#description}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Use the LTM AI-QEM function to estimate the expectation values of "
"observables for a given quantum circuit. To use LTM AI-QEM, provide a "
"quantum circuit, a set of observables to measure, and a QPU. The function "
"uses the same QPU time as the IBM Quantum Estimator primitive, and no "
"additional QPU time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The usual way to compute the expectation value of an observable $O$ is to "
"decompose the observable into a sum of Pauli operators, "
"$O=\\sum_i{c_i}{P_i}$, and calculate the expectation value of each Pauli "
"operator, $\\langle P_i \\rangle$, individually. The expectation value of "
"the observable, $\\langle O \\rangle$, is then the same linear combination "
"of the Pauli expectation values: ${\\langle O "
"\\rangle}=\\sum_i{c_i}{\\langle P_i \\rangle}$. If a set of Pauli operators "
"commute with each other, the measurements required to compute their "
"expectation values can be done simultaneously. When you run a circuit with "
"the LTM AI-QEM function, the function uses the Estimator primitive to run "
"the circuit on hardware as usual. It follows the same procedure of "
"decomposing the observable into Pauli operators and computing their "
"expectation values individually. Then the GCN-Transformer, a deep learning "
"architecture, predicts a correction factor $\\zeta_i$ for each Pauli "
"operator $P_i$. The function uses this correction factor to correct the "
"noisy expectation value of each Pauli operator: ${\\langle P_i "
"\\rangle}_{mitigated} = \\frac{{\\langle P_i \\rangle}_{noisy}}{\\zeta_i}$. "
"The mitigated expectation value is therefore ${\\langle O "
"\\rangle}_{mitigated} = \\sum_i{c_i}{\\langle {P_i} \\rangle}_{mitigated} = "
"\\sum_i{c_i}{\\frac{{\\langle P_i \\rangle}_{noisy}}{\\zeta_i}}$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The workflow for using the LTM AI-QEM function is as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Define your circuits and observables.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Run the LTM AI-QEM function by passing a circuit and observable pairs as "
"primitive unified blocs (PUBs).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Retrieve the results, which contain the expectation value of the observable "
"with respect to the corresponding quantum circuit in each PUB.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Many test examples empirically show that the LTM AI-QEM function improves "
"the estimation of observables. The function has the following features:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Utility-scale experiments:** Test examples show that AI-QEM performs "
"better than other AI-based methods for circuits with large depth and large "
"width. See the [Benchmarks](#benchmarks) section for results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Primitive:** The function supports the Estimator primitive, so you can use"
" it to compute the expectation value of any observable with respect to any "
"quantum circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Primitive unified bloc (PUB):** The LTM AI-QEM function supports all types"
" of PUBs (that is, all circuit-observable configurations) that the Estimator"
" primitive supports.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The AI-QEM technique begins with dataset construction, in which random "
"quantum circuits are generated with various parameter settings. For each "
"circuit, both the ideal expectation values (obtained through simulation) and"
" the noisy expectation values (obtained from hardware execution) are "
"computed. These results, along with the corresponding quantum circuits and "
"backend information, are stored in a dataset repository. The dataset is then"
" used to train a machine-learning or deep-learning model that can mitigate "
"noise in quantum computations, and the trained model is saved for future "
"use. During inference, the trained model takes as input a quantum circuit, "
"its noisy expectation value computed on hardware, the relevant observables, "
"and backend information. The model then processes these inputs to produce a "
"noise-mitigated expectation value.\n"
msgstr ""

