#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Example 1: GHZ circuit {#example-1-ghz-circuit}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This example shows the basic workflow of the LTM AI-QEM function with a "
"simple $n$-qubit GHZ circuit and the two-body tensor product observable "
"$Z_iZ_j$, whose expectation value for the GHZ state is known to be 1.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Construct the PUB {#construct-the-pub}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Like the Estimator primitive, the LTM AI-QEM function accepts primitive "
"unified blocs (PUBs) as input. A PUB is a tuple of the form `(circuit, "
"observables, parameter_values, precision)`, where `parameter_values` is "
"required for parametric circuits and `precision` is optional.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Circuit {#circuit}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The $n$-qubit Greenberger-Horne-Zeilinger (GHZ) state is given by:\n"
"$$\n"
"|GHZ\\rangle_{n}\n"
"= \\frac{\\vert{0}\\rangle^{\\otimes{n}} + \\vert{1}\\rangle^{\\otimes{n}}}{\\sqrt2}\n"
"= \\frac{\\overbrace{\\vert{00}\\dots{0}\\rangle}^{\\text{$n$ qubits}} + \\overbrace{\\vert{11}\\dots{1}\\rangle}^{\\text{$n$ qubits}}}{\\sqrt2}.\n"
"$$\n"
"To construct the $n$-qubit GHZ state, initialize all qubits in the $|0\\rangle$ state and apply a Hadamard gate to the first qubit to create an equal superposition. Then apply a sequence of CNOT gates across the entire register, with qubit $i$ as the control and qubit $i+1$ as the target. This staircase of entangling gates propagates the initial superposition to all qubits, which results in the entangled GHZ state.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Observables {#observables}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Measuring the two-body tensor product $Z_i \\otimes Z_j$ on a GHZ state "
"evaluates the parity correlation between any two qubits in the register. "
"Because the GHZ state is a superposition of the all-zeros and all-ones "
"states, any two qubits are perfectly correlated. The eigenvalue is $(+1 "
"\\times +1) = 1$ for the $\\vert00\\dots0\\rangle$ branch and $(-1 \\times "
"-1) = 1$ for the $\\vert11\\dots1\\rangle$ branch. Because both branches "
"return +1, the theoretical expectation value is 1. Construct the observable "
"with the `SparsePauliOp` class.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Because the circuit is not parametric, construct the PUB as `(circuit, "
"observable)`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/guides/ltm-ai-"
"qem/extracted-outputs/e4ac6fdf-1.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Run the LTM AI-QEM function {#run-the-ltm-ai-qem-function}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Run `ltm_function` by passing `pub` to it. You can check the status of the "
"job with `job.status()`, and get the job ID from the `job.job_id` "
"attribute.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Extract and visualize the results {#extract-and-visualize-the-results}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Extract the result with the `result()` method of `job`. Because the input is"
" a single PUB with a simple structure, extracting the unmitigated and "
"mitigated results is straightforward. Use `results[0].data.noisy_evs` and "
"`results[0].data.evs`, respectively, where `[0]` is the index of the only "
"`PubResult` that `result()` returns.\n"
msgstr ""

