#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Example 2: Mirror circuit {#example-2-mirror-circuit}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This example uses a mirror circuit to calculate expectation values of "
"observables passed in PUBs of different shapes. It demonstrates the LTM AI-"
"QEM function and its compatibility with different PUB shapes, and compares "
"the mitigated results with the unmitigated results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A mirror circuit applies a randomly generated circuit and then its inverse. "
"In an ideal, noiseless execution, the two halves cancel and return the "
"qubits to their initial state. This makes the circuit useful for studying "
"how hardware and runtime noise affect an otherwise predictable result.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In this example, you construct multiple PUBs. Each PUB pairs the same mirror"
" circuit with observables, made up of Pauli operators, that are given in "
"different shapes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The LTM AI-QEM function evaluates these observables and returns both the "
"expectation values from the noisy execution and the values after mitigation."
" This example has two goals: to verify that the function accepts the "
"supported PUB formats, and to compare the mitigated expectation values with "
"the noisy values to see how effectively the error mitigation workflow "
"restores the expected behavior of the mirror circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Construct the PUBs {#construct-the-pubs}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Each PUB is a tuple of a circuit and a list of observables. The function "
"computes the expectation values of the observables with respect to the "
"circuit. In this example, the circuit is the same for all of the observable "
"configurations. You pass the list of PUBs, `pubs`, to `ltm_function` to "
"run.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Circuit {#circuit-1}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"First, create a mirror circuit with five qubits. To do this, create a random"
" circuit with the Qiskit `random_circuit` function. Specify the number of "
"qubits (`n_qubits=5`), the circuit depth (`depth=5`), and a seed for "
"reproducibility (`seed=42`). Then add a `barrier` at the end of the random "
"circuit, and use `unitary_overlap` to create the mirror circuit. Because "
"`unitary_overlap(U1, U2)` creates the circuit that corresponds to "
"$U_{2}^{\\dagger}U_{1}$, passing the same circuit as both arguments creates "
"the mirror circuit. The `barrier` prevents the circuit from becoming the "
"identity during transpilation. The code output shows the depth of the mirror"
" circuit, which is expected to be about 10 for `depth=5`, plus the barrier "
"layers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Observables {#observables-1}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Next, create the lists of observables whose expectation values you want to "
"compute, in different formats. First, define the variables `ALL_X_PAULI = "
"XXXXX`, `ALL_Y_PAULI = YYYYY`, and `ALL_Z_PAULI = ZZZZZ`, which represent "
"the corresponding Pauli observables on all five qubits.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Then define `obs_1` as a list of three observables. The first is a linear "
"combination of two Pauli operators, $0.3\\,ZZZZZ + 0.5\\,XXXXX$, defined as "
"a `SparsePauliOp`. The second is `SparsePauliOp([ALL_Z_PAULI])`, also a "
"`SparsePauliOp`. The third is the Pauli string `ALL_X_PAULI`. The PUB with "
"`obs_1` has an observable array of shape `(3,)`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Finally, define `obs_2` in a matrix layout, that is, as a list of lists, "
"where each inner list contains observables of type `SparsePauliOp` or Pauli "
"strings. The first row contains `ALL_X_PAULI` and $0.3\\,ZZZZZ + "
"0.5\\,YYYYY$. The second row contains $0.3\\,XXXXX + 0.5\\,ZZZZZ$ and "
"$2.0\\,ZZZZZ$. The PUB with `obs_2` has an observable array of shape `(2, "
"2)`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Collect the two PUBs as `pubs = [(mirror_circuit, obs_1), (mirror_circuit, "
"obs_2)]`.\n"
msgstr ""

