#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Example 4: Brickwork Clifford circuit {#example-4-brickwork-clifford-"
"circuit}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This example simulates Clifford circuits. This family of circuits is useful "
"because you can design the simulation to have a known expectation value. "
"Specifically, you can design the circuit and the observable so that the "
"expectation value is always 1, as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**The starting point (the stabilizer):** Start with the vacuum state "
"$|0\\rangle^{{\\otimes}n}$. Applying the Pauli $Z$ or the identity operator "
"to $|0\\rangle$ leaves it unchanged: $Z|0\\rangle = |0\\rangle$ and $I "
"|0\\rangle = |0\\rangle$. Therefore, any Pauli string $P$ that is a tensor "
"product of only $Z$ and $I$ leaves the all-zero state "
"$|0\\rangle^{{\\otimes}n}$ unchanged: $P |0\\rangle^{{\\otimes}n} = "
"|0\\rangle^{{\\otimes}n}$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**The Clifford evolution:** Applying the unitary $U_L$ to the state gives "
"$|\\psi\\rangle = U_L |0\\rangle^{{\\otimes}n}$. To find an observable whose"
" expectation value for this new state is 1, apply the same unitary "
"transformation to the operator: $O_L = U_L P U_L^{\\dagger}$. This is the "
"Heisenberg picture of quantum mechanics.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Why the observable stays a single Pauli string:** If $U_L$ contained non-"
"Clifford gates (such as T gates or arbitrary rotations), $U_L P "
"U_L^\\dagger$ would expand into a sum of many different Pauli strings. You "
"would have to measure many different observables and add them to get 1. "
"However, because the circuit uses only Clifford gates, the Gottesman-Knill "
"theorem guarantees that $U_L P U_L^\\dagger$ maps to exactly one Pauli "
"string (for example, $X Y -Z X$).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '4. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**The mathematical guarantee:** Because of this construction, computing the "
"expectation value is a simple algebraic substitution:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle O_L \\rangle = \\langle \\psi | O_L | \\psi \\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Substitute $|\\psi \\rangle$ and $O_L$:\n"
"$$\n"
"(\\langle 0 |^{\\otimes n} U_L^{\\dagger})\n"
"(U_L P U_L^{\\dagger})\n"
"(U_L | 0 \\rangle ^{\\otimes n})\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The adjacent $U_L^\\dagger U_L$ terms cancel (they equal the identity $I$):\n"
"$$\n"
"= \\langle 0 |^{\\otimes n} P | 0 \\rangle ^{\\otimes n}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Because $P$ is a tensor product of only $Z$ and $I$, $P| 0 \\rangle ^{\\otimes n} = | 0 \\rangle ^{\\otimes n}$, and therefore:\n"
"$$\n"
"= \\langle 0 |^{\\otimes n} | 0 \\rangle ^{\\otimes n} = 1\n"
"$$\n"
"First, design quantum circuits that contain only Clifford gates: the single-qubit gates $H$, $S$, $X$, $Y$, and $Z$, and the two-qubit gate $CZ$. The `create_brickwork_clifford` function takes the number of qubits (`num_qubits`) and `depth` as arguments, and returns a Clifford quantum circuit with the given number of qubits and depth.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The circuits in this example have 50 qubits (`num_qubits=50`) and varying "
"depths: 30, 35, 40, 45, 50, 55, 60, 65, and 70. For the observables, start "
"from a Pauli string $P$ with Pauli weight 2, that is, two Pauli $Z$ "
"operators at random positions. Then use the `evolve` method to evolve $P$ "
"into $U_{L_i} P U_{L_i}^{\\dagger}$ for the circuit $U_{L_i}$ of each depth."
" Save the circuits and the corresponding observables in the lists "
"`circuits_list` and `observables_list`, respectively.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To get the ideal values, run a stabilizer simulation. Create a stabilizer "
"state from each quantum circuit with the Qiskit `StabilizerState` class, "
"which takes a `QuantumCircuit` as input and outputs the corresponding "
"stabilizer state. Then use the `expectation_value` method to calculate the "
"ideal expectation value, which by design is 1 for each circuit and "
"observable pair.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Next, create a list of PUBs named `clifford_pubs` and pass it to "
"`ltm_function` to compute the expectation values on hardware and mitigate "
"them with AI-QEM.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Extract and visualize the results {#extract-and-visualize-the-results-2}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"After the job finishes, collect the expectation values and standard "
"deviations from each PUB result. For every circuit depth, the results "
"include the noisy (unmitigated) values, `noisy_evs` and `noisy_stds`, and "
"the values after mitigation by AI-QEM, `mitigated_evs` and "
"`mitigated_stds`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Then use the `plot_clifford_results` function to plot the expectation value "
"against the circuit depth. The plot contains the following:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Unmitigated:** The raw expectation values returned by the hardware, shown "
"with error bars of size $\\pm\\sigma$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Mitigated (AI-QEM):** The expectation values after error mitigation, also "
"shown with error bars.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Ideal (stabilizer simulation):** The noiseless expectation value, "
"$\\langle O \\rangle_{\\text{ideal}} = 1$, for all depths.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Threshold:** A gray band that covers $-0.1 \\le \\langle O \\rangle \\le "
"0.1$. Values inside this band have no useful signal to mitigate, so they are"
" left unmitigated.\n"
msgstr ""

